QUESTIONS = [{'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Speed, Distance & Time', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'A train 150 meters long passes an electric pole in 15 seconds and crosses another train of the same length travelling in the opposite direction in 10 seconds. What is the speed of the second train?', 'option_a': '54 km/h', 'option_b': '72 km/h', 'option_c': '60 km/h', 'option_d': '90 km/h', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Speed of train 1 = Distance / Time = 150m / 15s = 10 m/s. When crossing the second train in opposite directions, relative speed = (150 + 150) / 10 = 300 / 10 = 30 m/s. Relative speed = S1 + S2 = 10 + S2 = 30 m/s, so S2 = 20 m/s. In km/h: 20 * (18 / 5) = 72 km/h.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Time & Work', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'A can finish a work in 18 days and B can do the same work in 15 days. B worked for 10 days and left the job. In how many days can A alone finish the remaining work?', 'option_a': '5 days', 'option_b': '5.5 days', 'option_c': '6 days', 'option_d': '8 days', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Let total work = LCM(18, 15) = 90 units. Rate of A = 90 / 18 = 5 units/day. Rate of B = 90 / 15 = 6 units/day. Work done by B in 10 days = 10 * 6 = 60 units. Remaining work = 90 - 60 = 30 units. Time taken by A = 30 / 5 = 6 days.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Percentages', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'If the price of sugar increases by 25%, by what percentage must a household reduce its consumption so that the total expenditure remains unchanged?', 'option_a': '20%', 'option_b': '25%', 'option_c': '16.67%', 'option_d': '30%', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Reduction percentage formula = [r / (100 + r)] * 100. Here r = 25%. Reduction = [25 / (100 + 25)] * 100 = (25 / 125) * 100 = 1/5 * 100 = 20%.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Profit & Loss', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'A shopkeeper sells two articles at Rs. 990 each. On one he gains 10% and on the other he loses 10%. What is his overall gain or loss percentage?', 'option_a': 'No profit, no loss', 'option_b': '1% gain', 'option_c': '1% loss', 'option_d': '2% loss', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'When two items are sold at the same selling price with x% gain on one and x% loss on the other, there is always an overall loss given by (x / 10)^2 percent. Here (10 / 10)^2 = 1% loss.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Simple & Compound Interest', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'The difference between compound interest and simple interest on a certain sum for 2 years at 10% per annum is Rs. 65. What is the principal sum?', 'option_a': 'Rs. 6,000', 'option_b': 'Rs. 6,500', 'option_c': 'Rs. 7,000', 'option_d': 'Rs. 7,200', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'For 2 years, Difference = P * (R / 100)^2. Here Difference = 65, R = 10%. So 65 = P * (10 / 100)^2 => 65 = P * (1 / 100) => P = 65 * 100 = Rs. 6,500.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Permutation & Combination', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': "In how many different ways can the letters of the word 'LEADING' be arranged so that the vowels always come together?", 'option_a': '360', 'option_b': '480', 'option_c': '720', 'option_d': '5040', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "In 'LEADING', vowels are E, A, I (3 vowels) and consonants are L, D, N, G (4 consonants). Treating the 3 vowels as one single unit gives 4 + 1 = 5 units. These 5 units can be arranged in 5! = 120 ways. Within the unit, the 3 vowels can be arranged in 3! = 6 ways. Total ways = 120 * 6 = 720."}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Probability', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'Two dice are thrown simultaneously. What is the probability of getting two numbers whose product is even?', 'option_a': '1/4', 'option_b': '1/2', 'option_c': '3/4', 'option_d': '5/6', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Total possible outcomes = 6 * 6 = 36. The product is odd only if both dice show odd numbers (1, 3, or 5). Number of odd-odd outcomes = 3 * 3 = 9. So P(product is odd) = 9 / 36 = 1/4. Therefore, P(product is even) = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Ratio & Proportion', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': 'The ratio of boys and girls in a college of 720 students is 7:5. How many more girls should be admitted so that the ratio becomes 1:1?', 'option_a': '90', 'option_b': '120', 'option_c': '150', 'option_d': '180', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Total parts = 7 + 5 = 12. 1 part = 720 / 12 = 60. Boys = 7 * 60 = 420. Girls = 5 * 60 = 300. For a 1:1 ratio, number of girls must equal number of boys (420). Girls to be added = 420 - 300 = 120.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Averages & Ages', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': "The average age of 24 students and their teacher is 15 years. If the teacher's age is excluded, the average age decreases by 1 year. What is the age of the teacher?", 'option_a': '38 years', 'option_b': '39 years', 'option_c': '40 years', 'option_d': '42 years', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "Total age of 25 persons (24 students + 1 teacher) = 25 * 15 = 375 years. When the teacher is excluded, remaining 24 students have average age = 15 - 1 = 14 years. Total age of 24 students = 24 * 14 = 336 years. Teacher's age = 375 - 336 = 39 years."}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Mixtures & Alligations', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'In what ratio must a grocer mix tea at Rs. 60 per kg and Rs. 65 per kg so that by selling the mixture at Rs. 68.20 per kg he may gain 10%?', 'option_a': '3:2', 'option_b': '3:4', 'option_c': '3:5', 'option_d': '4:5', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Selling price of mixture = Rs. 68.20 with 10% profit, so Cost Price of mixture = 68.20 / 1.10 = Rs. 62 per kg. By rule of alligation: (Price 1: 60, Price 2: 65, Mean: 62). Ratio = (65 - 62) / (62 - 60) = 3 / 2. Ratio is 3:2.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Number Systems', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'What is the remainder when (7^84) is divided by 342?', 'option_a': '1', 'option_b': '7', 'option_c': '49', 'option_d': '341', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Notice that 7^3 = 343 = 342 + 1. So 7^84 = (7^3)^28 = (342 + 1)^28. By the Binomial theorem, all terms except the last term (1^28) are multiples of 342. Thus remainder = 1^28 = 1.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Pipes & Cisterns', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'Pipe A can fill a tank in 12 hours and Pipe B can empty it in 18 hours. If both pipes are opened together in an empty tank, how long will it take to fill completely?', 'option_a': '24 hours', 'option_b': '30 hours', 'option_c': '36 hours', 'option_d': '48 hours', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Net part filled per hour = (1/12) - (1/18) = (3 - 2) / 36 = 1/36. Therefore, the empty tank will be filled in 36 hours.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Time & Work', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': '12 men or 18 women can do a piece of work in 14 days. In how many days will 8 men and 16 women finish the same work?', 'option_a': '8 days', 'option_b': '9 days', 'option_c': '10 days', 'option_d': '12 days', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': '12 men = 18 women => 1 man = 1.5 women. Thus, 8 men = 8 * 1.5 = 12 women. So 8 men + 16 women = 12 + 16 = 28 women. Using W1 * D1 = W2 * D2: 18 * 14 = 28 * D => D = (18 * 14) / 28 = 18 / 2 = 9 days.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Speed, Distance & Time', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'A person travels from city A to city B at a speed of 60 km/h and returns from B to A at 40 km/h. What is the average speed for the entire journey?', 'option_a': '48 km/h', 'option_b': '50 km/h', 'option_c': '52 km/h', 'option_d': '54 km/h', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'When distances are equal, Average speed = (2 * S1 * S2) / (S1 + S2) = (2 * 60 * 40) / (60 + 40) = 4800 / 100 = 48 km/h.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Boats & Streams', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'A boat can travel with a speed of 13 km/h in still water. If the speed of the stream is 4 km/h, find the time taken by the boat to go 68 km downstream.', 'option_a': '3 hours', 'option_b': '4 hours', 'option_c': '5 hours', 'option_d': '5.5 hours', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Speed downstream = Speed in still water + Speed of stream = 13 + 4 = 17 km/h. Time taken = Distance / Speed = 68 / 17 = 4 hours.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'HCF & LCM', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': 'The H.C.F. and L.C.M. of two numbers are 11 and 7700 respectively. If one of the numbers is 275, find the other number.', 'option_a': '279', 'option_b': '283', 'option_c': '308', 'option_d': '318', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Product of two numbers = HCF * LCM. Let the other number be X. 275 * X = 11 * 7700 => X = (11 * 7700) / 275 = 7700 / 25 = 308.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Logarithms', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'If log(x) + log(x - 3) = 1, what is the value of x (base 10)?', 'option_a': '5', 'option_b': '2', 'option_c': '-2', 'option_d': '10', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'log(x * (x - 3)) = 1 => x(x - 3) = 10^1 = 10. Expanding: x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0 => (x - 5)(x + 2) = 0. Since log is defined only for positive numbers, x cannot be -2. Therefore, x = 5.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Geometry & Mensuration', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'The diagonal of a rectangle is 25 cm and its area is 168 cm^2. What is the perimeter of the rectangle?', 'option_a': '56 cm', 'option_b': '62 cm', 'option_c': '70 cm', 'option_d': '84 cm', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Let length = l, breadth = b. Given l^2 + b^2 = 25^2 = 625, and lb = 168. We know (l + b)^2 = (l^2 + b^2) + 2lb = 625 + 2(168) = 625 + 336 = 961. So (l + b) = sqrt(961) = 31 cm. Perimeter = 2(l + b) = 2 * 31 = 62 cm.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Percentages', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': 'In an examination, 70% of students passed in English, 65% passed in Mathematics, and 27% failed in both subjects. If 248 candidates passed in both subjects, what was the total number of candidates?', 'option_a': '350', 'option_b': '400', 'option_c': '500', 'option_d': '600', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Percentage failing in at least one subject = 100% - (passed both). Or: % failed in English = 30%, % failed in Maths = 35%, % failed both = 27%. Total % failing in at least one = 30 + 35 - 27 = 38%. Percentage passing in both = 100 - 38 = 62%. Given 62% of Total = 248 => Total = (248 * 100) / 62 = 400.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Clocks & Calendars', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': 'What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand of a clock at 3:40?', 'option_a': '120 degrees', 'option_b': '130 degrees', 'option_c': '140 degrees', 'option_d': '150 degrees', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Angle formula = |(30 * H) - (11/2 * M)|. Here H = 3, M = 40. Angle = |(30 * 3) - (11/2 * 40)| = |90 - 220| = |-130| = 130 degrees.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Clocks & Calendars', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'If 1st January 2007 was a Monday, what day of the week was 1st January 2008?', 'option_a': 'Monday', 'option_b': 'Tuesday', 'option_c': 'Wednesday', 'option_d': 'Sunday', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'The year 2007 is an ordinary year (not a leap year), so it has 365 days = 52 weeks + 1 odd day. Therefore, the day advances by 1. Monday + 1 = Tuesday.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Profit & Loss', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Zoho', 'question': 'A dishonest dealer professes to sell his goods at cost price, but uses a weight of 900 grams for a 1 kg weight. Find his actual gain percentage.', 'option_a': '10%', 'option_b': '11.11%', 'option_c': '12.5%', 'option_d': '9.09%', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Gain percentage = [Error / (True Value - Error)] * 100 = [100g / (1000g - 100g)] * 100 = (100 / 900) * 100 = 100 / 9 = 11.11%.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Syllogisms', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Statements: (1) All mangoes are fruits. (2) Some fruits are sweet.\\nConclusions:\\nI. Some mangoes are sweet.\\nII. No mango is sweet.', 'option_a': 'Only conclusion I follows', 'option_b': 'Only conclusion II follows', 'option_c': 'Either I or II follows', 'option_d': 'Neither I nor II follows', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "Since the middle term 'fruits' is not distributed in both premises, no definite conclusion can be drawn between mangoes and sweet. However, conclusion I ('Some mangoes are sweet') and conclusion II ('No mango is sweet') form a complementary pair (I and E type). Therefore, either I or II must follow."}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Blood Relations', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': "Pointing to a photograph of a boy, Suresh said, 'He is the only son of my mother.' How is Suresh related to that boy?", 'option_a': 'Brother', 'option_b': 'Father', 'option_c': 'Uncle', 'option_d': 'Himself', 'answer': 'D', 'explanation': "Suresh's mother's only son is Suresh himself. Since the photograph is of that only son, the boy in the photograph is Suresh himself."}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Blood Relations', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'A is the father of C and D is the son of B. E is the brother of A. If C is the sister of D, how is B related to E?', 'option_a': 'Daughter', 'option_b': 'Sister-in-law', 'option_c': 'Sister', 'option_d': 'Wife', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "A is the father of C and D. Since C and D are siblings, and D is the son of B, B must be the mother of C and D, and therefore B is the wife of A. E is the brother of A. Thus, B (A's wife) is E's sister-in-law."}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Coding-Decoding', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': "In a certain code language, 'SYSTEM' is written as 'SYSMET' and 'NEARER' is written as 'AENRER'. How is 'FRACTION' written in that code?", 'option_a': 'CARFNOIT', 'option_b': 'NOITCARF', 'option_c': 'CARFTINO', 'option_d': 'CRAFOITN', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "Divide the 8-letter word 'FRACTION' into two halves of 4 letters each: 'FRAC' and 'TION'. Reverse the letters of each half: 'FRAC' reversed becomes 'CARF', and 'TION' reversed becomes 'NOIT'. Combining both yields 'CARFNOIT'."}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Direction Sense', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'Rahul walks 30 meters towards North, then turns right and walks 40 meters. He then turns right and walks 20 meters, and again turns right and walks 40 meters. How far and in which direction is he from his original position?', 'option_a': '10 meters North', 'option_b': '10 meters South', 'option_c': '20 meters North', 'option_d': '50 meters East', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Vertical net displacement: 30m North - 20m South = 10m North. Horizontal net displacement: 40m East - 40m West = 0m. He is exactly 10 meters North of his starting point.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Seating Arrangement', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Five friends P, Q, R, S, and T are sitting facing North. S is between T and Q. Q is to the immediate left of R. P is to the immediate left of T. Who is sitting in the middle?', 'option_a': 'P', 'option_b': 'Q', 'option_c': 'S', 'option_d': 'T', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'P is to the immediate left of T -> (P, T). S is between T and Q -> (P, T, S, Q). Q is to the immediate left of R -> (P, T, S, Q, R). The 5 positions from left to right are P, T, S, Q, R. S is exactly in the middle.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Number & Letter Series', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': 'Find the next term in the series: 3, 12, 27, 48, 75, 108, ?', 'option_a': '135', 'option_b': '147', 'option_c': '156', 'option_d': '162', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Notice: 3 = 3 * 1^2, 12 = 3 * 2^2, 27 = 3 * 3^2, 48 = 3 * 4^2, 75 = 3 * 5^2, 108 = 3 * 6^2. The next term is 3 * 7^2 = 3 * 49 = 147.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Statement & Assumption', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': "Statement: 'Please consult a doctor before taking any sleeping pills.' - Notice on a medicine pack.\\nAssumptions:\\nI. People often take sleeping pills without medical advice.\\nII. Taking sleeping pills without consultation can be hazardous.", 'option_a': 'Only assumption I is implicit', 'option_b': 'Only assumption II is implicit', 'option_c': 'Neither I nor II is implicit', 'option_d': 'Both I and II are implicit', 'answer': 'D', 'explanation': 'A cautionary notice is placed because people tend to consume over-the-counter pills without guidance (Assumption I is implicit), and because unsupervised intake poses health hazards (Assumption II is implicit). Both are implicit.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Analogies', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'Odometer is to Mileage as Compass is to:', 'option_a': 'Speed', 'option_b': 'Hiking', 'option_c': 'Needle', 'option_d': 'Direction', 'answer': 'D', 'explanation': 'An odometer is an instrument used to measure mileage. Similarly, a compass is an instrument used to determine direction.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Venn Diagrams', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'Which of the following diagrams best represents the relationship among: Musicians, Instrumentalists, and Pianists?', 'option_a': 'Three disjoint separate circles', 'option_b': 'Three concentric circles (Pianists inside Instrumentalists inside Musicians)', 'option_c': 'Two intersecting circles inside a third circle', 'option_d': 'Three mutually intersecting circles', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'All pianists are instrumentalists, and all instrumentalists are musicians. Therefore, Pianists is a subset of Instrumentalists, which is a subset of Musicians. They form three concentric circles.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Odd One Out', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'Choose the number which does NOT belong to the group: 28, 65, 126, 215, 342', 'option_a': '65', 'option_b': '126', 'option_c': '215', 'option_d': '342', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Pattern: n^3 + 1. 28 = 3^3 + 1, 65 = 4^3 + 1, 126 = 5^3 + 1, 342 = 7^3 + 1. But 215 = 6^3 - 1 (216 - 1). Therefore, 215 is the odd one out.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Data Sufficiency', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'Question: Who among A, B, C, D and E is the tallest?\\nStatement I: D is taller than A and C, but shorter than B.\\nStatement II: E is shorter than B but taller than D.', 'option_a': 'Statement I alone is sufficient', 'option_b': 'Statement II alone is sufficient', 'option_c': 'Both statements I and II together are sufficient', 'option_d': 'Neither statement is sufficient', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'From Statement I: B > D > (A, C). From Statement II: B > E > D. Combining both: B is taller than E, D, A, and C. Thus, B is definitely the tallest among all five. Both statements together are necessary and sufficient.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Cause & Effect', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Statement I: The municipal corporation announced a 20% water cut in the city for the next 3 days.\\nStatement II: A major pipeline burst occurred near the primary water reservoir yesterday.', 'option_a': 'Statement I is the cause and II is its effect', 'option_b': 'Statement II is the cause and I is its effect', 'option_c': 'Both statements are independent causes', 'option_d': 'Both statements are effects of independent causes', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'The pipeline burst near the water reservoir (Statement II) is the direct cause that led the municipal corporation to announce the 20% water cut (Statement I).'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Mathematical Operations', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': "If '+' means '*', '-' means '/', '*' means '+', and '/' means '-', evaluate: 16 + 4 * 10 - 2 / 8", 'option_a': '55', 'option_b': '61', 'option_c': '65', 'option_d': '70', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Substitute symbols: 16 * 4 + 10 / 2 - 8. By BODMAS order: 10 / 2 = 5; 16 * 4 = 64; 64 + 5 - 8 = 69 - 8 = 61.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Critical Reasoning', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': 'In a company, all top performers receive bonuses. Some bonus recipients are promoted to managers. If Priya is a top performer, which of the following MUST be true?', 'option_a': 'Priya is promoted to manager', 'option_b': 'Priya receives a bonus', 'option_c': 'Priya will never be promoted', 'option_d': 'Priya is the head of the department', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "Since ALL top performers receive bonuses, and Priya is a top performer, she must receive a bonus. Whether she is among the 'some' who are promoted to manager is possible but not guaranteed to be true."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Vocabulary (Synonyms)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': "Choose the word most nearly similar in meaning to 'PRAGMATIC':", 'option_a': 'Theoretical', 'option_b': 'Practical', 'option_c': 'Arrogant', 'option_d': 'Careless', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'Pragmatic' refers to dealing with things sensibly and realistically in a way that is based on practical rather than theoretical considerations. Synonym: Practical."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Vocabulary (Antonyms)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': "Choose the word most nearly opposite in meaning to 'EPHEMERAL':", 'option_a': 'Fleeting', 'option_b': 'Transient', 'option_c': 'Permanent', 'option_d': 'Volatile', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "'Ephemeral' means lasting for a very short time. 'Fleeting' and 'Transient' are synonyms. The antonym is 'Permanent'."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Spotting Errors', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': "Identify the part of the sentence containing a grammatical error:\\n'Neither the manager (A) / nor the employees (B) / was present at (C) / the annual general meeting. (D)'", 'option_a': 'Neither the manager', 'option_b': 'nor the employees', 'option_c': 'was present at', 'option_d': 'the annual general meeting', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "When two subjects are joined by 'neither... nor', the verb must agree in number with the closer subject. Here the closer subject is plural ('the employees'), so the verb must be 'were present', not 'was present'. Error is in part C."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Spotting Errors', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': "Identify the incorrect part:\\n'One of the most important factor (A) / for achieving success (B) / is maintaining consistent discipline (C) / over a long period. (D)'", 'option_a': 'One of the most important factor', 'option_b': 'for achieving success', 'option_c': 'is maintaining consistent discipline', 'option_d': 'over a long period', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "The phrase 'One of the...' is always followed by a plural noun. It should be 'One of the most important factors', not 'factor'. Error is in part A."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Sentence Completion / Fillers', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'Despite facing severe financial adversity, he _______ in his efforts to complete his higher education.', 'option_a': 'persisted', 'option_b': 'relented', 'option_c': 'hesitated', 'option_d': 'stagnated', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "'Despite facing severe financial adversity' sets up a contrast showing perseverance. 'Persisted' (continued firmly in an action despite difficulty) fits logically and grammatically."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Para Jumbles', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Rearrange the sentences into a coherent paragraph:\\nP: It enables machines to learn from empirical data.\\nQ: Artificial Intelligence has transformed modern industries.\\nR: Consequently, automated decision-making has become highly accurate.\\nS: Machine Learning serves as the primary engine behind this transformation.', 'option_a': 'Q - S - P - R', 'option_b': 'S - P - Q - R', 'option_c': 'Q - P - S - R', 'option_d': 'R - Q - S - P', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Q introduces the broad topic (AI transforming modern industries). S narrows it down to the engine behind it (Machine Learning). P explains what Machine Learning does (enables machines to learn from data). R provides the concluding result (automated decision-making becomes accurate). Order: Q - S - P - R.'}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Idioms & Phrases', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': "What is the meaning of the idiom 'To burn the candle at both ends'?", 'option_a': 'To be very wasteful with money', 'option_b': 'To work exhaustingly hard from early morning until late night', 'option_c': "To lose one's temper in public", 'option_d': 'To illuminate an entire room efficiently', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'To burn the candle at both ends' means to exhaust oneself by working or partying very hard from early in the morning until late at night without getting adequate rest."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'One Word Substitution', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': "Give one word for: 'A person who does not believe in the existence of God.'", 'option_a': 'Agnostic', 'option_b': 'Atheist', 'option_c': 'Altruist', 'option_d': 'Ascetic', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "An 'Atheist' is someone who disbelieves or lacks belief in the existence of God. (An 'Agnostic' believes nothing is known about God's existence; an 'Altruist' is unselfishly concerned for others; an 'Ascetic' practices severe self-discipline)."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Reading Comprehension', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': "Passage: 'Renewable energy adoption has accelerated globally due to technological breakthroughs that have reduced the per-megawatt cost of solar and wind generation below that of fossil fuels. However, grid reliability requires substantial investments in energy storage and distributed transmission networks.'\\nWhat is the primary obstacle to fully relying on renewables mentioned in the passage?", 'option_a': 'Higher per-megawatt production costs compared to coal', 'option_b': 'Public resistance to wind turbines', 'option_c': 'Requirement of massive investments in storage and grid transmission', 'option_d': 'Total lack of technological breakthroughs', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "The text states: 'However, grid reliability requires substantial investments in energy storage and distributed transmission networks', highlighting storage and transmission infrastructure as the primary challenge."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Active & Passive Voice', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': "Convert into Passive Voice: 'The research team has published the groundbreaking findings.'", 'option_a': 'The groundbreaking findings were published by the research team.', 'option_b': 'The groundbreaking findings have been published by the research team.', 'option_c': 'The groundbreaking findings had been published by the research team.', 'option_d': 'The groundbreaking findings are being published by the research team.', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "Present perfect tense ('has published') in active voice transforms to 'have/has been + past participle' in passive voice. Since 'the groundbreaking findings' is plural, the correct form is 'have been published'."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Direct & Indirect Speech', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': "Convert into Indirect Speech: Ananya said, 'I will complete the project by tomorrow.'", 'option_a': 'Ananya said that she will complete the project by tomorrow.', 'option_b': 'Ananya said that she would complete the project by the next day.', 'option_c': 'Ananya said that she would be completing the project tomorrow.', 'option_d': 'Ananya said that she had completed the project the previous day.', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "In indirect speech, the reporting verb is in past tense ('said'), so 'will' changes to 'would', 'I' changes to 'she', and 'tomorrow' changes to 'the next day'."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Spelling Correction', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'Select the correctly spelled word:', 'option_a': 'Conscientious', 'option_b': 'Conscensious', 'option_c': 'Conscientous', 'option_d': 'Conscintious', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "The correct spelling is 'Conscientious' (wishing to do one's work or duty thoroughly and well)."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Idioms & Phrases', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': "Choose the correct meaning of 'To call a spade a spade':", 'option_a': 'To indulge in gardening', 'option_b': 'To speak frankly and directly without sugarcoating', 'option_c': 'To play cards skillfully', 'option_d': 'To be deceptive in negotiations', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'To call a spade a spade' means to describe something directly and truthfully, even if it is unpleasant or blunt."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Data Structures', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': 'What is the worst-case time complexity of searching for an element in a balanced Binary Search Tree (BST) with N nodes?', 'option_a': 'O(1)', 'option_b': 'O(log N)', 'option_c': 'O(N)', 'option_d': 'O(N log N)', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'In a balanced BST (such as an AVL or Red-Black Tree), the height of the tree is bounded by O(log N). In each step of a search, half of the remaining sub-tree is eliminated, resulting in a worst-case time complexity of O(log N).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Python Fundamentals', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'What will be the output of the following Python snippet?\\n\\na = [1, 2, 3]\\nb = a\\nb.append(4)\\nprint(len(a))', 'option_a': '3', 'option_b': '4', 'option_c': 'TypeError', 'option_d': 'None', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'In Python, lists are mutable objects. The assignment `b = a` assigns the reference of list `a` to `b` (no new copy is made). Appending 4 to `b` modifies the same underlying list in memory. Therefore, `len(a)` is 4.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'C Language & Pointers', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Zoho', 'question': 'What is the output of the following C code snippet?\\n\\nint arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\\nint *p = arr;\\nprintf("%d ", *(p++));\\nprintf("%d", *p);', 'option_a': '10 10', 'option_b': '10 20', 'option_c': '20 20', 'option_d': '20 30', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'The expression `*(p++)` dereferences the pointer before incrementing it due to post-increment postfix rules. So the first printf prints `arr[0]` which is 10. Then `p` points to `arr[1]`. The second printf prints `*p` which is 20. Output: 10 20.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'DBMS & SQL', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': 'Which SQL clause is used to filter records resulting from an aggregate function like SUM() or COUNT()?', 'option_a': 'WHERE', 'option_b': 'GROUP BY', 'option_c': 'HAVING', 'option_d': 'ORDER BY', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'The WHERE clause cannot be used with aggregate functions because WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation occurs. The HAVING clause was added to SQL specifically to filter aggregated summary rows.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Object-Oriented Programming', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'Which OOP principle focuses on wrapping code and data together into a single unit while restricting direct access to internal details?', 'option_a': 'Inheritance', 'option_b': 'Polymorphism', 'option_c': 'Encapsulation', 'option_d': 'Abstraction', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Encapsulation is the mechanism that binds together code and the data it manipulates, and keeps both safe from outside interference and misuse (e.g. through private fields and public getters/setters).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Operating Systems', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': "Which of the following is NOT one of Coffman's four necessary conditions for a Deadlock to occur?", 'option_a': 'Mutual Exclusion', 'option_b': 'Hold and Wait', 'option_c': 'Preemption allowed', 'option_d': 'Circular Wait', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "The four Coffman conditions for deadlock are: (1) Mutual Exclusion, (2) Hold and Wait, (3) No Preemption (resources cannot be preempted), and (4) Circular Wait. Therefore, 'Preemption allowed' actually prevents deadlock, making C the correct answer."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Algorithms (Sorting)', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'Which of the following sorting algorithms has a guaranteed worst-case time complexity of O(N log N) and is stable?', 'option_a': 'Quick Sort', 'option_b': 'Heap Sort', 'option_c': 'Merge Sort', 'option_d': 'Selection Sort', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Merge Sort always divides the array into halves and merges in O(N), guaranteeing O(N log N) time in best, average, and worst cases. Additionally, standard Merge Sort preserves the relative order of equal elements (it is stable). Heap Sort is O(N log N) but not stable; Quick Sort has worst case O(N^2).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Computer Networks', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': 'At which OSI reference model layer does the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) operate?', 'option_a': 'Network Layer', 'option_b': 'Transport Layer', 'option_c': 'Session Layer', 'option_d': 'Data Link Layer', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'TCP and UDP are transport layer protocols that provide end-to-end communication services for applications.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Data Structures (Stacks & Queues)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'Which data structure follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle and is commonly used in function call call-stacks and undo mechanisms?', 'option_a': 'Queue', 'option_b': 'Stack', 'option_c': 'Linked List', 'option_d': 'Binary Tree', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'A Stack operates on the LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) principle, where the most recently added item is the first one removed.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Java Fundamentals', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': "In Java, what happens when you compare two String objects using the '==' operator?", 'option_a': 'It compares the character contents of both strings', 'option_b': 'It compares their memory references (object identity)', 'option_c': 'It causes a compilation error', 'option_d': 'It compares the string lengths', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "In Java, the '==' operator compares whether two references point to the exact same memory location in the heap/pool. To compare the actual textual contents of two strings, the `.equals()` method must be used."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Data Structures', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': 'What is the average time complexity of inserting a key-value pair into a Hash Table that uses separate chaining?', 'option_a': 'O(1)', 'option_b': 'O(log N)', 'option_c': 'O(N)', 'option_d': 'O(N^2)', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'With a good hash function that uniformly distributes keys across buckets, each bucket contains an average of O(1) elements (assuming constant load factor). Thus, insertion takes O(1) on average. (Worst case when all keys collide is O(N)).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Python Fundamentals', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'What is the output of `[x**2 for x in range(5) if x % 2 == 1]` in Python?', 'option_a': '[1, 4, 9]', 'option_b': '[1, 9]', 'option_c': '[0, 1, 4, 9, 16]', 'option_d': '[1, 9, 25]', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': '`range(5)` generates numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. The condition `x % 2 == 1` filters odd numbers: 1 and 3. The squares are 1^2 = 1 and 3^2 = 9. Output: [1, 9].'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'DBMS & SQL', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'Which Normal Form eliminates transitive functional dependency in relational database design?', 'option_a': 'First Normal Form (1NF)', 'option_b': 'Second Normal Form (2NF)', 'option_c': 'Third Normal Form (3NF)', 'option_d': 'Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': '1NF removes repeating groups; 2NF removes partial dependency on composite candidate keys; 3NF removes transitive dependencies (where a non-prime attribute depends on another non-prime attribute).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'C++ Fundamentals', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Zoho', 'question': 'Which C++ keyword is used to declare a function in a base class that can be overridden in derived classes to achieve runtime polymorphism?', 'option_a': 'static', 'option_b': 'virtual', 'option_c': 'override', 'option_d': 'friend', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "Declaring a member function with the `virtual` keyword instructs the compiler to use dynamic binding (via a vtable/vptr) so that the derived class's implementation is invoked at runtime."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Operating Systems', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'What phenomenon occurs when excessive page swapping causes an operating system to spend more time paging than executing actual instructions?', 'option_a': 'Spooling', 'option_b': 'Thrashing', 'option_c': 'Starvation', 'option_d': 'Segmentation fault', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "Thrashing occurs when a computer's virtual memory subsystem is in a constant state of paging, rapidly exchanging data in memory for data on disk, degrading CPU performance to near zero."}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Cryptarithmetic & Puzzles', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'In the cryptarithmetic addition: SEND + MORE = MONEY, each distinct letter represents a unique single digit from 0 to 9. What is the value of the letter M?', 'option_a': '0', 'option_b': '1', 'option_c': '2', 'option_d': '9', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'In the 4-digit addition SEND + MORE = MONEY, the sum produces a 5-digit number MONEY. The carry-over from adding two 4-digit numbers can at most be 1. Since M is the leading digit of the sum, M cannot be 0, so M must be 1.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Probability', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'A bag contains 6 black and 8 white balls. One ball is drawn at random. What is the probability that the ball drawn is white?', 'option_a': '3/7', 'option_b': '4/7', 'option_c': '1/8', 'option_d': '3/4', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Total balls = 6 + 8 = 14. White balls = 8. Probability = 8 / 14 = 4 / 7.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Simple Interest', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'A sum of money doubles itself in 8 years at simple interest. What is the rate of interest per annum?', 'option_a': '10%', 'option_b': '12.5%', 'option_c': '15%', 'option_d': '16.67%', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Let principal = P. If it doubles, amount = 2P, so Simple Interest SI = P. SI = (P * R * T) / 100 => P = (P * R * 8) / 100 => R = 100 / 8 = 12.5% per annum.'}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Time & Work', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': 'A and B together can do a piece of work in 12 days. B and C together can do it in 15 days. C and A together can do it in 20 days. In how many days can A, B, and C together finish the work?', 'option_a': '8 days', 'option_b': '10 days', 'option_c': '12 days', 'option_d': '15 days', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "2(A + B + C)'s 1 day work = 1/12 + 1/15 + 1/20 = (5 + 4 + 3) / 60 = 12/60 = 1/5. Therefore, (A + B + C)'s 1 day work = 1/10. Thus, together they can finish the work in 10 days."}, {'category': 'Aptitude', 'topic': 'Ratio & Proportion', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'Two numbers are in the ratio 3 : 5. If 9 is subtracted from each, the new numbers are in the ratio 12 : 23. What is the smaller number?', 'option_a': '27', 'option_b': '33', 'option_c': '45', 'option_d': '55', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'Let numbers be 3x and 5x. (3x - 9) / (5x - 9) = 12 / 23. 23(3x - 9) = 12(5x - 9) => 69x - 207 = 60x - 108 => 9x = 99 => x = 11. The smaller number is 3x = 3 * 11 = 33.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Verbal Reasoning (Statement & Course of Action)', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Statement: Every year during the monsoon season, several areas of the city experience severe waterlogging and traffic paralysis.\nCourses of Action:\nI. The civic body should thoroughly clear and desilt all storm-water drains before the onset of monsoon.\nII. All citizens should be advised not to step out of their homes during the entire rainy season.', 'option_a': 'Only course of action I follows', 'option_b': 'Only course of action II follows', 'option_c': 'Either I or II follows', 'option_d': 'Both I and II follow', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Course of action I is a practical, constructive preventive measure. Course II is impractical and disproportionate (asking citizens not to step out during an entire season is unfeasible). Hence, only I follows.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Verbal Reasoning (Syllogisms)', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'Statements: Some papers are pens. All pens are scales. No scale is a pencil.\nConclusions:\nI. No pen is a pencil.\nII. Some papers are scales.', 'option_a': 'Only conclusion I follows', 'option_b': 'Only conclusion II follows', 'option_c': 'Neither I nor II follows', 'option_d': 'Both conclusions I and II follow', 'answer': 'D', 'explanation': 'Since all pens are scales and no scale is a pencil, no pen can ever be a pencil (Conclusion I is valid). Since some papers are pens and all pens are scales, those papers that are pens are definitely scales (Conclusion II is valid). Both I and II follow.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Verbal Reasoning (Assertion & Reason)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Wipro', 'question': 'Assertion (A): Leaves of plants appear green.\nReason (R): Chlorophyll absorbs green light and reflects red and blue light.', 'option_a': 'Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A', 'option_b': 'Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A', 'option_c': 'A is true but R is false', 'option_d': 'A is false but R is true', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'Leaves appear green because chlorophyll absorbs blue and red wavelengths of light and REFLECTS green light. Hence, Assertion A is true, but Reason R is scientifically false.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Coding-Decoding', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': 'In a certain code, MONKEY is written as XDJMNL. How will TIGER be written in that code?', 'option_a': 'QDFHS', 'option_b': 'SDFHS', 'option_c': 'SHFDQ', 'option_d': 'UJHFS', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Reverse the word MONKEY to YEKNOM, then subtract 1 from each letter: Y-1=X, E-1=D, K-1=J, N-1=M, O-1=N, M-1=L => XDJMNL. For TIGER, reversed is REGIT. Subtract 1 from each letter: R-1=Q, E-1=D, G-1=F, I-1=H, T-1=S => QDFHS.'}, {'category': 'Reasoning', 'topic': 'Seating Arrangement (Circular)', 'difficulty': 'Hard', 'company': 'Capgemini', 'question': 'Six persons A, B, C, D, E, and F are sitting in a circle facing the center. B is between F and C. A is between E and D. F is to the immediate left of D. Who is sitting opposite to A?', 'option_a': 'B', 'option_b': 'C', 'option_c': 'E', 'option_d': 'F', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Arrange clockwise: D, F, B, C, E, A. D is adjacent to F. B is between F and C. E is next to C. A is between E and D. The opposite pairs are (D, C), (F, E), and (A, B). Therefore, B is sitting directly opposite to A.'}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Sentence Correction', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'Choose the grammatically correct sentence:', 'option_a': 'No sooner did the bell rang than the students rushed out.', 'option_b': 'No sooner did the bell ring than the students rushed out.', 'option_c': 'No sooner the bell rang when the students rushed out.', 'option_d': 'No sooner had the bell rung when the students rushed out.', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'No sooner did' takes the base form of the verb ('ring', not 'rang') and must be followed by 'than', not 'when'. Option B is correct."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Vocabulary (Synonyms)', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': "Select the word that is most nearly identical in meaning to 'METICULOUS':", 'option_a': 'Hasty', 'option_b': 'Painstaking', 'option_c': 'Superficial', 'option_d': 'Indifferent', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'Meticulous' means showing great attention to detail; very careful and precise. 'Painstaking' is an exact synonym."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Vocabulary (Antonyms)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': "Choose the antonym of 'CANDID':", 'option_a': 'Frank', 'option_b': 'Honest', 'option_c': 'Deceitful', 'option_d': 'Straightforward', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': "'Candid' means truthful, outspoken, and straightforward. The antonym is 'Deceitful' (guarded, dishonest)."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'One Word Substitution', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Deloitte', 'question': 'One who possesses multiple diverse talents and abilities is termed as:', 'option_a': 'Versatile', 'option_b': 'Veteran', 'option_c': 'Philanthropist', 'option_d': 'Ambivert', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "'Versatile' means able to adapt or be adapted to many different functions or activities; having many talents."}, {'category': 'Verbal', 'topic': 'Idioms & Phrases', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': "What does the idiom 'A blessing in disguise' signify?", 'option_a': 'A curse that ruins everything', 'option_b': 'Something that seems bad at first, but turns out to have good results', 'option_c': 'A person wearing a spiritual costume', 'option_d': 'A secret gift from a stranger', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "'A blessing in disguise' is an apparent misfortune that eventually results in something good or advantageous."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Pseudocode & Bitwise Logic', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Accenture', 'question': 'What is the output of the following pseudocode?\n\nInteger a, b, c\nSet a = 5, b = 3\nc = (a ^ b) + (a & b)\nPrint c', 'option_a': '6', 'option_b': '7', 'option_c': '8', 'option_d': '15', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': 'In binary: a = 5 (101_2), b = 3 (011_2). a ^ b = 101 ^ 011 = 110_2 = 6. a & b = 101 & 011 = 001_2 = 1. Therefore, c = 6 + 1 = 7.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Recursion & Stack', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'Infosys', 'question': 'What value is returned by fun(4)?\n\nint fun(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * fun(n - 1);\n}', 'option_a': '10', 'option_b': '16', 'option_c': '24', 'option_d': '120', 'answer': 'C', 'explanation': 'This is the standard recursive factorial function. fun(4) = 4 * fun(3) = 4 * 3 * fun(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * fun(1) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24.'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Data Structures (Arrays)', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Amazon', 'question': 'What is the time complexity to access an element by its index in a contiguous dynamic array?', 'option_a': 'O(1)', 'option_b': 'O(log N)', 'option_c': 'O(N)', 'option_d': 'O(N^2)', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': 'Arrays are stored in contiguous memory blocks. The address of any element at index i is calculated directly using base_address + i * element_size, allowing direct random access in constant time O(1).'}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'DBMS & SQL', 'difficulty': 'Medium', 'company': 'TCS', 'question': 'Which of the following ACID properties ensures that a transaction is completely executed or completely rolled back in case of a crash?', 'option_a': 'Atomicity', 'option_b': 'Consistency', 'option_c': 'Isolation', 'option_d': 'Durability', 'answer': 'A', 'explanation': "Atomicity states that database modifications must follow an 'all or nothing' rule. If one part of the transaction fails, the entire transaction fails and the database state is left unchanged."}, {'category': 'Programming', 'topic': 'Python Fundamentals', 'difficulty': 'Easy', 'company': 'Cognizant', 'question': "What will be the output of `bool('')` and `bool([0])` in Python?", 'option_a': 'False, False', 'option_b': 'False, True', 'option_c': 'True, False', 'option_d': 'True, True', 'answer': 'B', 'explanation': "An empty string '' is falsy, so bool('') evaluates to False. A list with any element, even [0], is non-empty, so bool([0]) evaluates to True."}]

def get_all_questions():
    return QUESTIONS

def get_questions_by_category(category):
    return [q for q in QUESTIONS if q['category'].lower() == category.lower()]

def get_questions_by_company(company_name):
    target = company_name.lower()
    return [q for q in QUESTIONS if target in q['company'].lower() or q['company'].lower() == 'all']
